\begin_layout Subsection
Alzheimer's Disease
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One of the most infamous neurodegenrative diseases, which is also the most
 common cause of dementia
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "who_dementia_2026"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, is Alzheimer's Disease (AD).
 Its definition keeps changing over the years, yet it is increasingly recognized
 as a heterogeneous, multifactorial and multifaceted disorder, associated
 with neurological, psychiatric, and systemic comorbidities.
 It operates across several interconnected domains, including cognitive-behavior
al symptomatology, underlying biological mechanisms, varying neuroanatomical
 networks, and complex genetic variability.
 These vast complexity and heterogenity are the root cause of the ongoing
 struggle to understand the disease and develop suitable treatments for
 different patients.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To give a short background of AD research, historically, research and clinical
 diagnosis have focused heavily on core hallmark biomarkers, like the abnormal
 extracellular accumulation of Amyloid-Beta (
\begin_inset Formula $A\beta$
\end_inset

) protein plaques and the intracellular aggregation of hyperphosphorylated
 Tau protein tangles
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jack_tracking_2013"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 While
\begin_inset Formula $A\beta$
\end_inset

 often marks early pathological changes, the spatial spread of Tau pathology
 correlates more directly with localized neurodegeneration and cognitive
 decline.
 Adding to this complexity is genetic variability and genetic profiles which
 are known to influence the disease, such as carrying the Apolipoprotein
 E4 (APOE4) allele, which stands out as the strongest genetic risk factor,
 significantly accelerating pathology onset and altering disease progression
 across different patient populations
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kakkar_comprehensive_2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Traditional diagnostic and predictive frameworks usually rely on limited-fields
 mechanisms, such as the biological markers we mentioned, cellular characeterist
ic and inter-cellular interactions, behavioral or anatomical charateristics,
 or genetic factors.
 Understanding the interplay between these diverse changes under AD is essential
 to define and identify disease phenotypes for targeted interventions, all
 while available disease data tends to be noisy, sparse and spread across
 entirely different scales and measures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, a suitable unifying methodology/framework is required to handle
 said diversity.
 Such framework should be capable of connecting the different faces of the
 disease and generate reliable, extensive predictions of how diverse factors
 drive disease progression.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Metamodeling Approach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This presents a computational challenge.
 Forcing every dimension of the AD pathology into a single mathematical
 representation is inherently impractical.
 We suggest and examine here a divide-and-conquer strategy, which is the
 
\series bold
bayesian metamodeling
\series default
.
 It is based on utilizing smaller, specialized models that each capture
 a particular facet of AD, and then fuse/couple them together under a bayesian
 probabilistic framework - the metamodel
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "raveh_bayesian_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This strategy allows a highly adaptive model.
 The metamodel can continously evolve alongside new clinical and biological
 disoveries, on two fronts: first, by having the flexibility to change,
 update or replace the individual submodels themselves; and second, by dynamical
ly adjusting the probablistic connections between the submodels, which are
 based on current scientific knowledge
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "neve-oz_bayesian_2024"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Thus, discoveries from one specific field can influence the mathematical
 constraints of other domains, ensuring that our computational tools can
 continuously adapt to the growing complex knowledge of the disease - which
 makes this strategy highly suitable to the multifaceted and heterogenous
 problem of AD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Exploring the bayesian metamodeling strategy offers computational and modeling
 advantages for analyzing any complex system, not limited to AD, diseases
 or even biology in general.
 It overcomes the rigidity of monolithic architectures, by allowing different
 computational approaches, such as mechanistic equations and data-driven
 algorithm to interact and enrich each other under a unifying framework.
 Furthermore, understanding the use of probablistic tools provides a benefical
 way to manage uncertainty and reduce expensive models computations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here, we attempted to create a metamodel of characterization and prediction
 of AD progression in patients that combines two partial models, corresponding
 to two different essential aspects of understanding the disease for specific
 patients.
 The first partial model describes the temporal dynamics, kinetic rates
 and clinical decline rates of biomarkers
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bossa_multidimensional_2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

; and the second identifies distinct spatiotemporal trajectories of cortical
 tau deposition to stratify patients into phenotypic subtypes and progression
 trajectories
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vogel_four_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Both models are probabilistic, and we aim to show that formulated whithin
 a probabilistic framework, the unified metamodel combines their distinct
 representations to calibrate prediction certainties and infereces, yielding
 more robust and reliable forecasts of AD progression.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We explored the bayesian metamodling approach for Alzheimer's Disease progressio
n using two known models out of the literature.
 In order to utilize them in the bayesian metamodeling framework, we converted
 each model to a probablistic surrogate model, so we can couple them, infer
 and sample in a joint probabilistic space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus the bayesian metamodeling process we did can be summarized to 3 stages:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Individual Model Surrogate Training
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Creating a Coupling Architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 Sample and infer from the joint coupled probablistic framework
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
1.
 Individual Model Surrogate Training
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
General
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each surrogate model we used is a fast, probabilistic emulator that replaces
 an expensive computational model with a probability density function.
 Mathematicaly, it models the conditional probability distirbution of the
 model outputs given the inputs:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}|\boldsymbol{x}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}$
\end_inset

 - inputs: parameters, initial conditions, patient baseline characteristic
 and measurments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Theta}$
\end_inset

 - outputs: predicted future states, disease proggression coefficients,
 biomarkers levels, classification probabilities, etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus the trained surrogate evaluates the probability density of a given
 prediction 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Theta}$
\end_inset

 and can also draw samples out of the conditioned model's output distribution
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Theta}\sim p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}|\boldsymbol{x}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The surrogate model could be obtained by any approach for modeling statistical
 distribution, where the major tradeoffs between methods are often expressivenes
s and interpretability
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "raveh_bayesian_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Since we are dealing with complex models, we chose to focus on the expressivity
 of the surrogates and implemented both surrogates using modern simulation
 based inference (SBI
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cranmer_frontier_2020"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) methods.
 Specifically, using the original model we created a large set of pairs
 of inputs 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}$
\end_inset

 and their corresponding outputs 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\theta}$
\end_inset

, which created the training dataset.
 Then, we used a neural posterior estimator (NPE) to fit a deep generative
 neural network directly to the dataset.
 This network approxiamtes the true distribution of the model 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}|\boldsymbol{x}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Full simulations outputs might be high-dimensional and complex, and their
 internal mechanics might make full-state probability modeling computationally
 hard/intractable.
 Therefore, selecting specific inputs and outputs for the surrogate to model
 can serve as an essential solution that defines a compact and usable probablist
ic description.
 The surrogates only needs to learn the essential causal relationships of
 the original complex model's inputs-outputs which are relevant to the broader
 metamodel.
 The selection of variables for the surrogate is meaningful, as it establishes
 what information propagates within the metamodel, and should be done with
 the later coupling and forward and backward propagation of information
 between models in mind.
\end_layout

