\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
First Model - The ODE model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first and main model is the ODE-based model of AD presented by Bossa
 and Sahli in 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

A multidimensional ODE-based model of Alzheimer’s disease progression
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bossa_multidimensional_2023"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It uses longitudinal differential equations to track a state vector of
 continuous disease-burden variables, like Amyloid-Beta, Tau, and cognitive
 domain scores.
 Using personalized bayesian fitting, the model infers probability distributions
 over the differential eqution rate matrix from the patient's data, estimating
 each paramaer as a distribution rather than a single fixed value.
 It then uses these parameters to forecast future trajectories of the variables.
 Importantly, it also uses an ordered logistic GLM to calculate a weighted
 disease burden score, which is used to assign probabilities for clinical
 diagnostic of the AD severity: Cognitive Normal (CN), Mild Cognitive Impairment
 (MCI) and Dementia (AD).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Simply put, its inputs are a patient's demographics and their observerd
 longitudinal biomarker and cognitive measurements; and its outputs are
 the estimated rate distributions, and possibly the trajectory forecast
 for future biomarkers levels and clinical diagnostic probabilities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To replace it with a probablistic surrogate model, we chose to focus only
 on some of the parameters:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Surrogate inputs:
\series default
 Age, APOE4 status, Amyloid-Beta baseline + 2-year measure, Tau baseline
 + 2-year measure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Surrogate outputs: 
\series default
Tau self-dynamics rate, Amyloid-Beta self-dynamics rate, Amyloid-Beta affect
 on Tau rate, cognitive memory result baseline + 5 year, clinical stage
 result baseline + year.
\end_layout

