\begin_layout Paragraph
Second Model - The SuStaIn model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second model we used is the spatial progression model presented by Vogel
 et al.
 in "Four distinct trajectories of tau deposition identified in Alzheimer’s
 disease"
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vogel_four_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It is a subtype and stage inference (SuStaIn
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "young_uncovering_2018"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) model that focuses on the biomarker Tau protein.
 It maps cross-sectional biomarker accumulation across multiple brain regions
 into distinct temporal pathways (subtypes) and sequential disease severity
 stages.
 Given sufficient training data, using Bayesian MCMC, the model automatically
 discovers the distinct trajectory sequences and calculates a patient's
 probabilistic placement across these subtypes and stages.
 Crucially, rather than assuming a single uniform progression path, it estimates
 posterior probability distributions for a patient belonging to each subtype
 and occupying each stage of pathology.
 That is, it firsts uses the entire training data to infer and characterize
 the different possible subtypes of Tau progression/trajectory in AD, and
 then it can be used to assign patients to a subtype of Tau trajectory and
 their stage in it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Simply put, its inputs are a patient's cross-sectional regional Tau measurments
 across different brain regions; and its outputs are the posterior probability
 distributions for belonging to each disease subtype, and being at each
 discrete progression stage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To replace it with a simpler probabilistic surrogate model, we chose to
 focus on the regional Tau scores and collapsed the discrete stage probabilities
 into a continous expected value that represents the stage within the trajactory
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Surrogate inputs:
\series default
 Regional Tau PET Z-scores across brain regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Surrogate outputs:
\series default
 A probability of belonging to each of the subtypes, and a single continuous
 expected spatial stage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here as well we sucessully trained the neural posterior estimator as a surrogate
 model.
 We used simulated data with 7 stages for each subtypes and 3 Tau trajectory
 subtypes overall.
 We can observe its results and performances in the graphs below.
