\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The General Coupling Framework
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to fuse the models and couple them inside one metamodel framework,
 we had to make them operate in the same probability space: instead of calculati
ng the probabilties of the outputs of the ODE-model (
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE}|\boldsymbol{X}_{ODE}\right)$
\end_inset

) and the SuStaIn model (
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}|\boldsymbol{X}_{SuStaIn}\right)$
\end_inset

) separately and independently, we needed to calculate their joint probability:
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}|\boldsymbol{X}_{shared}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To do so, we created links between different variables of the two surrogate
 models.
 Those links push the models variables to agree - whether both models handle
 the same variable and they are pushed to agree on its value; or that a
 variable from one model is mathematically/evidently related to another,
 and their values should sustain a certain relation or link.
 The coupling links are things we force on the surrogates, which originate
 from a meta view using real-world knowledge and biological and medical
 premises or conclusions from other researches.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Those coupling links (
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

) act as constraints on the joint distribution of the surrogates, and their
 probabilities depend on their outputs.
 That is, the greater the alignment between the two sub-models, the greater
 the probability that the coupling link constraint is satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This allows us, after applying the coupling constraints, to recalculate
 the joint probability distribution function, using Bayes rule:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying the constraints, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)$
\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}|C\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Using Bayes and our assumption that both surrogates are independent at
 first (two different models (we omitted the obvious conditions of the inputs
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{X}$
\end_inset

 for comfort):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right) & =\frac{p\left(C|\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)\cdot p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)}{p\left(C\right)}\\
 & \propto p\left(C|\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)\cdot p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE}\right)\cdot p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Since we can calculate both 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE}\right)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)$
\end_inset

 with the surrogate models, it remains to define the probability score 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(C|\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right):=\phi\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)$
\end_inset

 .
 This can be done in various ways given to our choice, and this is where
 we defined the coupling itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example, if we wanted to make two specific different variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{ODE},\theta_{SuStaIn}$
\end_inset

 to agree on their values, we could define a gaussian link between them,
 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)=\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}\left(\theta_{ODE};\theta_{SuStaIn},\sigma\right)=\ensuremath{\mathcal{N}}\left(\theta_{SuStaIn};\theta_{ODE},\sigma\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 determines the strength of the coupling.
 Clearly the probability increases where they are similar and decreases
 where the variables are far part; and the bigger 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 the greater is the decrease in probability score for each difference size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice that we can concatenate different couplings, so that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)=\phi_{1}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)\cdot...\cdot\phi_{n}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After that, for any evaluation or sampling method we have over the entire
 data we want to infer about, we can evaluate it using at least three scores:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The probability scores of the output as is calculated by each surrogate
 model separately.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The probability score given by the coupling constraint we defined.
\end_layout

