\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Coupling Links
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We used three coupling links between the two models, two Gaussians links
 that operate on transformation of different variables from both surrogate
 models, and one other kind of link between different variables (a 'directional
 potential' link).
 The linked variables can be viewed in Figure 7.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph

\series bold
Directional Potential Link - Subtype-Driven Velocity/Tau Dynamics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Coupled variables from ODE-model:
\series default
 Tau self rate (entry 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{11}$
\end_inset

 in the ODE rate matrix)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Coupled Variable from SuStain model:
\series default
 Subtypes probabilities (probability of subtype 0, probability of subtype
 1, probability of subtype 2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Different real-world subtypes of Tau trajectories corresponded with different
 Tau self spread rates
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vogel_four_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, so we infered 
\series bold
there should be a link between the probabilities of the subtype the SuStaIn
 model outputs, and the Tau rate variable/entry in the ODE-model's output
 of the rate matrix
\series default
.
 In Vogel et al.'s research four subtypes were found, whereas in our simulated
 data three subtypes were found.
 Based on the main shared characteristic of our subtypes and the research's,
 we decided to which subtypes we want to relate with a general faster rate
 (subtypes 0, 2) and to what degree, and which with a slower Tau spread
 rate (subtype 1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the values of the spread rate and the probabilities of subtypes are
 completely unrelated, there was no logic to make them or a transformation
 of them linked with a gaussian to push toward similarity.
 Therefore we constructed the coupling constraint such that a bigger lean
 towards the faster subtypes rewards probabilistically bigger Tau rates,
 and vice versa.
 Further analysis of the coupling is viewed under Methods.
 We named this score a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

directional potential
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is important to note that there wasn't a profound experimental base to
 the magnitude of the score we assigined for each subtype, and the scores
 and weights should be tested against massive real patients data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Gaussian Link 1 - Disease Stage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Coupled variables from ODE-model:
\series default
 clinical stage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Coupled Variable from SuStain model:
\series default
 (Tau spread) expected stage, Subtypes probabilities (probability of subtype
 0, probability of subtype 1, probability of subtype 2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An increasing SuStaIn stage was strongly associated with worse global cognition
 and with a more advanced AD clinical diagnose
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vogel_four_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 T
\series bold
herefore, a higher probability for a higher Tau expected stage should be
 linked with a higher probability for a worse clinical stage
\series default
 (CN to AD).
 Furthermore, since different subtypes experience the same Tau spread magnitude
 in different degrees in different areas, the subtype also has a relation
 to the decrease in cognitive decline and different cognitive skills.
 Since in the current surrogate models we chose to focus on the memory decline
 (rather than language or praxis), we needed the link between the Tau stage
 and the clinical stage to factor that.
 I.e.
 a subtype that is related to memory-areas (subtype 1 in our case) should
 be related to a faster decline in earlier stages.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore we created a gaussian link between the clinical stage outputed
 by the ODE model, and a transformation of the SuStaIn model's Tau stage,
 weighted by the subtypes probabilities to give a bigger score for a bigger
 decline for patients with the appropriate decline.
 The transformation was required since we cannot directly link the clinical
 stage which is in range of 
\begin_inset Formula $[0.0,2.0]$
\end_inset

 to the Tau stage which is in range of 
\begin_inset Formula $[1,N]$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the maximal number of stages defined by the SuStaIn model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{2}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(clinicalStage_{ODE};f_{2}\left(expectedStage_{SuStaIn},subtypeProbs_{SuStaIn}\right),\sigma_{2}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once again, the sepcific mathematical transformation we used (and is elaborated
 in Methods) isn't a result of a thourough research on massive real data,
 but more of a reasonable trial-and-error on simulated data, and can and
 should be revised in the future.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Gaussian Link 2 - Clinical Subtype Prior
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Coupled variables from ODE-model:
\series default
 APOE4 status, Tau self rate (
\begin_inset Formula $v_{11}$
\end_inset

), cognitive memory score
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Coupled Variable from SuStain model:
\series default
 Subtypes probabilities (probability of subtype 0, probability of subtype
 1, probability of subtype 2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While the SuStaIn model predicts the probabilities for the patient's belonging
 to the different subtypes purely based on Tau PET brain scans, Vogel et
 al.
 showed each subtype has its own prominent characteristics, both of the
 disease progression and of the patient demographics as well, for example
 the gene they carry
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vogel_four_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Therefore we concluded there should be a link between other patients characater
itics given by the ODE-model to the probabilities given by the SuStaIn model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Based on real world findings, we defined a formula to calculate the probabilitie
s for each subtype based on data from the ODE model - where for example
 carrying the APOE4 gene increases the proability for certain subtypes,
 and better memory abilitiies increase the probability of another (see Methods
 for elaboration).
 Then we defined a gaussian coupling between these raw calculated probabilities
 and the model-given subtypes probabilities.
 Thus an allignment with certain subtype characteritic increases the score
 where the probability given to it is greater:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi_{3}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)=\mathcal{N}\left(subtypeProbs_{SuStaIn};f_{3}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE}\right),\sigma_{3}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here as well, the formula we used to calculate the probabilties, though
 based on real conclusion, should be revised and modified upon testing the
 metamodel with massive real data.
\end_layout

