\begin_layout Subsection
3.
 Sampling and Inferring from the Joint Coupled Framework
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After we established the coupling between the two models, we used the combined
 metamodel to sample and infer about patients, instead of using each model
 separaetly.
 This was done using a Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) algorithm, specifically
 Random Walk Metropolis, which compiles both models and the couplings into
 a unified probabilistic graph.
 This is because our surrogate models, although expressie, use neural networks
 which are like a black-box for us, and we can't use to our benefit the
 exact shape and kind of the distributions themselves.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given patients data 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{X}$
\end_inset

 we want to infer about - whether we want to get either the variables, i.e.
 the original models outputs, with the highest probability, or to get the
 means, we need to sample from the joint distribution.
 At evert step, candidate values for all model variables across both subsystems
 are proposed.
 Instead of calculating the probability score itself, the compiler calculates
 the total joing log-probability score (which allows products to become
 sum).
 Since the joint probability given the coupling is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}|\right)\propto p_{ODE}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE}\right)\cdot p_{SuStaIn}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)\cdot\prod_{i=1}^{3}\phi_{i}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)
\]

\end_inset

 we get that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
log\ p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)=logp_{ODE}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE}\right)+p_{SuStaIn}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)+\sum_{i=1}^{3}\phi_{i}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)+Constant
\]

\end_inset

 Therefore, all the compiler needs to do is to sum the individual surrogate
 log-priors with the coupling log-likelihood penalties/rewards.
 This way, the MCMC compiler balances the priors with the couplings we defined.
 If the surrogates predict plausible values but the coupling penalty is
 high, the overall score drops and this state will be rejected, which drives
 the MCMC away from that parameter region, until it reaches an area that
 is both plausible and reconciles with the biological coupling we forced.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore, because MCMC samples over this unified joint distribution,
 our metamodel can naturally handle incomplete patient profiles.
 If inputs for one of the models are missing (e.g., having regional PET scans
 but no longitudinal clinical history), information flows bidirectionally
 across the couplings to infer the unmeasured model's parameters and cross-predi
ct its outputs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We simulated a prediction for 2 patients, and we defined their data profiles
 to represent two distinct patient profiles - one aimed for conflict between
 the models, and one for amplification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph

\series bold
Defining the patients and the Expectations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
For Subject 1
\series default
, we defined a direct contradiction between the spatial presentation and
 the temporal/genetic characteristic: A patient that should have a fast-progress
ive disease, but its sapatial features match a subtype with a slower progression
 (subtype 1).
 We also added a contradictory between the clinical stage given by the ODE
 which we aimed for to be not advanced, and a relatively advanced Tau progressio
n stage from the SuStain model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using each model separately to infer, we expected first the SuStaIn model
 to predict a hight probability for subtype 1, and for the ODE model to
 predict a high Tau progression rate along with a not yet advanced clinical
 stage.
 However when predicting using the metamodel, we expected the contradiction
 to reduce the confidence in the original subtype classification in favor
 of another that suits a faster progression, and that simultaneously the
 ODE model's clinical forecast to be dragged worse by the advanced Tau stage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
For Subject 2
\series default
, we defined a profile that align with both models, where spatial and temporal/g
enetic features mutually reinforce each other.
 We aimed for the ODE model to have a severe state which is expressed in
 high biomarkers with a fast progression that is related to a certain Tau
 subtype (subtype 0); and for the SuStaIn model to have a severe Tau stage
 with spatial features that also relate to that subtype.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We expected the coupling metamodel to predict yet the same scores and prediction
, but amplified.
 That is - increase the proabbility of high and severe scores and the certainty/
probability for subtype 0, as well as lower the variance for other parameters
 that align between the two models, since they reinforce each other.
\end_layout

