\begin_layout Subsection
1.
 The Sub-models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Bossa and Sahli ODE Disease Progression Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The original ODE model uses longitudinal patient data to map the continuous-time
 cascade of Alzheimer's disease biomarkers via differential equations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Model Variables, Inputs and Outputs:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The inputs to the ODE model consist of baseline patient features—specifically
 patient Age, binary APOE4 genetic status, and initial baseline biomarker/cognit
ive measurements:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(0)=\left[A\beta,Tau,Language,Memory,Praxis\right]
\]

\end_inset

The continuous temporal progression of these five latent disease burden
 variables is governed by a linear system of ordinary differential equations:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{dx\left(t\right)}{dt}=A\cdot\left(x(t)-v_{0}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

 is an offset vector and 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $5\times5$
\end_inset

 patient-specific rate velocity matrix computed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=v+Age\cdot w_{Age}+APOE4\cdot w_{APOE}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 represents the baseline 
\begin_inset Formula $5\times5$
\end_inset

 interaction matrix (where diagonal elements 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{ii}$
\end_inset

 dictate intrinsic self-accumulation rates and off-diagonal elements
\begin_inset Formula $v_{ij}$
\end_inset

 dictate driving forces of 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, such as Amyloid driving Tau accumulation), while 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{Age}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{APOE}$
\end_inset

 are demographic and genetic sensitivity matrices that modulate these accumulati
on rates based on patient age and APOE4 status.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By solving this linear system analytically using matrix exponentials, the
 model generates three primary types of outputs:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

(1) continuous multi-year trajectory forecasts for future biomarker levels
 and cognitive component scores (such as 2-year and 5-year Memory deficits),
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

(2) inferred personalized kinetic rate constants extracted directly from
 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 (such as tau_self_dynamic 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{11}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

(3) diagnostic category probabilities for Cognitively Normal, MCI, and Dementia,
 summarized into a continuous expected clinical stage score 
\begin_inset Formula $CS\in\left[0.0,2.0\right]$
\end_inset

 using an ordered logistic GLM.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
How it works:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Training (Learning the Equations): During the initial training phase (via
 MCMC), the model computes a probability distribution for every coefficient
 in the differential equations.
 This is done on an as large as possible database of AD patients, where
 each must be with at least two different measurement times (in gaps of
 months or years).
 Each coefficient represents exactly how one biological variable influences
 another.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We then solved the ODE system forward in time via matrix exponentials to
 simulate 2-year and 5-year biomarker trajectories.
 These synthetically generated trajectories established our baseline model
 dynamics and were later used as the dataset for surrogate model training.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Causal Constraints: The researchers explicitly built biological facts into
 these equations.
 For example, the model dictates that Amyloid Beta drives Tau pathology,
 and Tau pathology drives Memory decline.
 Importantly, it enforces that the reverse is not true (e.g., worsening memory
 does not cause Tau to accumulate; the coefficient for that is constrained
 to zero).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Personalization: Once the population-level coefficient distributions are
 established, the model can take a specific patient's current data and fit
 an updated, personalized parameter distribution just for them.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Prediction: Only after calculating these personalized differential equation
 parameters can the model simulate the patient's trajectory forward in time
 to output forecasts for clinical classification and future biomarkers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Note:
\series default
 While Bossa and Sahli originally trained their population differential
 equation parameters using real longitudinal patient data from the Alzheimer’s
 Disease Neuroimaging Initiative (ADNI) cohort, our project relied entirely
 on synthetically generated patient profiles.
 Rather than using real clinical patient records, we synthetically constructed
 4,000 patient profiles by randomly sampling baseline features (uniform
 Age between 60–90 and binary APOE4 status) and drawing initial baseline
 states (
\begin_inset Formula $c_{0}$
\end_inset

) from pre-computed population posterior parameter matrices (
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{Age}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{APOE}$
\end_inset

 ,
\begin_inset Formula $v_{0}$
\end_inset

).
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \space{}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To compute the clinical stage 
\begin_inset Formula $CS$
\end_inset

, the model uses a linear predictor 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta$
\end_inset

 which represents the patient's overall disease load, calculated as a weighted
 combination of their biomarker values:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\eta=\sum_{j=1}^{5}x_{j}(t)\cdot\beta_{j}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using this linear predictor, the model calculates the probability of the
 patient belonging to each of three ordered clinical categories (1 = Cognitively
 Normal (CN), 2 = Mild Cognitive Impairment (MCI), 3 = Dementia (AD)) using
 cutpoint thresholds 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{2}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
P\left(CN\right) & =sigmoid\left(c_{1}-\eta\right)\\
P\left(MCI\right) & =sigmoid\left(c_{2}-\eta\right)-sigmoid\left(c_{1}-\eta\right)\\
P\left((AD\right) & =1-sigmoid\left(c_{2}-\eta\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Then, to represent this probabilistic classification as a continuous variable
 suitable for normalizing flows and regression links, the model computes
 the Expected Clinical Stage Score:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
CS=0\cdot P\left(CN\right)+1\cdot P\left(MCI\right)+2\cdot P\left(AD\right)
\]

\end_inset

This score smoothly maps the patient's condition to the clinical categories:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0.0,0.5\right]$
\end_inset

 CN
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\ensuremath{(0.5,1.5]}$
\end_inset

 MCI
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
(1.5, 2.0] AD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Vogel et al.
 Subtype and Staging Inference (SuStaIn) Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The original SuStaIn model uses longitudinal or cross-sectional patient
 data to map the temporal progression of disease biomarkers into distinct
 trajectories (subtypes).
 It models how different regions of the brain accumulate pathology (e.g.,
 Tau) over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Model Variables, Inputs and Outputs:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The SuStaIn model, in general, regardless to the specific AD use, is a spatiotem
poral disease progression framework designed to untangle disease heterogeneity
 from cross-sectional data.
 Its inputs consist of continuous regional biomarker measurements—here specifica
lly Tau PET Z-scores 
\begin_inset Formula $z=\left[z_{1},...,z_{N}\right]$
\end_inset

 measured across 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 distinct brain regions (such as the Entorhinal cortex, Precuneus, and Amygdala).
 Disease progression within each subtype 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in\left\{ 0,...,S-1\right\} $
\end_inset

 is modeled as an ordered sequence of discrete z-score threshold-crossing
 events (e.g., Z-scores reaching 1.0, 2.0, or 3.0 in specific regions), dividing
 spatial advancement into 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{stages}$
\end_inset

 sequential stages, where 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{stages}$
\end_inset

 is a hyperparameter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given a patient's Z-score scan 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{z}$
\end_inset

 SuStaIn evaluates the likelihood 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(\boldsymbol{z}|s,k\right)$
\end_inset

 of observing that spatial pattern at stage 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 of subtype 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 Applying Bayes' rule with learned population subtype prevalence fractions
\begin_inset Formula $f_{s}$
\end_inset

 and a uniform prior over stages, the model computes a 2D Joint Posterior
 Probability Matrix:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P\left(s,k|\boldsymbol{z}\right)=\frac{P\left(\boldsymbol{z}|s,k\right)\cdot f_{s}}{\sum_{s'=0}^{S-1}\sum_{k'=0}^{N_{stages}}P\left(\boldsymbol{z}|s',k'\right)f_{s'}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Summing this matrix across stages yields the Subtype Probabilities, while
 summing across subtypes yields the Stage Probabilities.
 To enable compatibility with flow-based surrogate neural networks, we collapsed
 the discrete stage vector into a single continuous scalar Expected Stage:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
ExpectedStage=\sum_{k=0}^{N_{stages}}k\cdot P\left(Stage\ k\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
How the Model Works
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Model training is performed on un-staged cross-sectional population datasets
 using a hierarchical optimization framework that combines Expectation-Maximizat
ion (EM) clustering with Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) sampling.
 The EM step finds maximum-likelihood estimates for the regional event sequence
 of each subtype and their population prevalence fractions 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{s}$
\end_inset

.
 Subsequently, MCMC sampling draws thousands of candidate event sequences
 and fractions to quantify model uncertainty, while Cross-Validation Information
 Criterion (CVIC) selects the optimal number of subtypes 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 During inference on a new patient scan 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{z}$
\end_inset

, SuStaIn bypasses trajectory optimization and directly evaluates the conditiona
l likelihood 
\begin_inset Formula $P\left(\boldsymbol{z}|s,k\right)$
\end_inset

 against the pre-trained MCMC sequence samples, returning instant, patient-speci
fic subtype classification probabilities and spatial stage distributions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Note:
\series default
 For our project implementation, rather than using restricted real-world
 PET scan databases, we utilized the official synthetic data simulator provided
 in the authors' pySuStaIn open-source repository
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "vogel_four_2021"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The simulator generates cross-sectional Z-score measurements across 7 key
 brain regions: the Entorhinal cortex, Amygdala, Parahippocampal gyrus,
 Fusiform gyrus, Inferior Temporal gyrus, Middle Temporal gyrus, and Precuneus.
 Because each of the 7 regions evaluates 3 distinct Z-score threshold levels
 (1.0, 2.0, and 3.0), the progression timeline is discretized into exactly
 21 total spatial stages (0 to 21), where Stage 0 represents a healthy baseline
 and Stage 21 represents complete spatial saturation across all regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We fitted the pySuStaIn algorithm across candidate models ranging from 1
 to 3 subtypes and evaluated model performance using Cross-Validation Informatio
n Criterion (CVIC).
 The 3-subtype model achieved the lowest CVIC score, proving mathematically
 optimal for capturing the spatial variance of the dataset.
 The algorithm identified three distinct tau progression trajectories:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Subtype 0 -
\series default
 
\series bold
Neocortical-Leaning/Atypical
\series default
 (36.3% population prevalence): An atypical pathway originating outside the
 limbic center in the Precuneus and temporal neocortex, clinically linked
 to early executive and visuospatial deficits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Subtype 1 - Classic Limbic
\series default
 (49.5% population prevalence): The traditional Braak pathway originating
 in the medial temporal lobe (Entorhinal cortex and Amygdala), characterized
 by early memory impairment and a slower, plateauing overall progression
 rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Subtype 2 - Cortical-Leaning Variant
\series default
 (14.2% population prevalence): A secondary neocortical variant originating
 in the Middle Temporal gyrus that spreads rapidly across the cortex, exhibiting
 fast accumulation kinetics similar to Subtype 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Implementing the Surrogate Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before surrogate training begins, the computational simulator (whether the
 ODE differential equation solver or the pySuStaIn algorithm) is run over
 thousands of forward simulations across representative physiological parameter
 ranges.
 This creates a tabular training dataset consisting of paired input-output
 vectors:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{D}=\left\{ \left(\boldsymbol{x}^{(1)},\boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(1)}\right),...,\left(\boldsymbol{x}^{(N)},\boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(N)}\right)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

where
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{x}$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
conditioning inputs
\series default
: Independent variables, initial conditions, baseline measurements, or demograph
ic drivers (e.g., patient age, APOE4 status, or baseline regional Z-scores).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Theta}$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
target outputs:
\series default
 Dependent outcomes, rate parameters, future forecasts, or classification
 probabilities (e.g., kinetic rate constants, 5-year memory deficit, spatial
 subtype probabilities, or expected stage).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Surrogate training is executed using the bayesian_metamodeling (bayemm)
 library framework written by Dr.
 Barak Raveh
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "raveh_metamodeler_codex_scaffold_docs"
literal "false"

\end_inset

, via its sbi_npe (Simulation-Based Inference via Neural Posterior Estimation)
 backend.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Instead of fitting a standard deterministic regression model (which would
 predict only a single average value and lose uncertainty), the framework
 fits a deep generative neural network that models the entire conditional
 probability density 
\begin_inset Formula $p\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}|\boldsymbol{x}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NPE is a class of Simulation-Based Inference (SBI) algorithms designed to
 estimate conditional probability distributions directly from simulated
 dataset pairs without requiring an explicit, tractable analytical likelihood
 function from the simulator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To represent complex, multi-modal, non-Gaussian probability distributions
 p(y∣x), NPE utilizes Normalizing Flows, and the specific architecture we
 used with out backend is a Masked Autoregressive Flow (MAF).
 It factorizes the joint conditional density of the outputs into a product
 of 1D conditional distributions using the chain rule of probability.
 To compute this efficiently in a single forward neural network pass, MAF
 applies binary masks to the weight matrices of standard feedforward neural
 networks (Masked Autoencoder for Distribution Estimation, MADE).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The neural network weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 are trained by minimizing the negative log-likelihood (NLL) over the dataset
 using Adam optimization:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{L}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}\right)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}log\ p_{MAF}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}^{(i)}|\boldsymbol{x}^{(i)};\theta\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once NLL loss converges, the backend library bayesmm serializes the trained
 neural network weights, feature normalization scalers, and input/output
 schemas into json files.
 During joint metamodel sampling, these saved artifacts are loaded as drop-in
 probabilistic emulators, enabling instant density evaluation and sample
 generation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
ODE-model Surrogate Training
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To train the ODE surrogate model we constructed a 4,000-sample dataset by
 integrating the differential equations forward in time using matrix exponential
s from pre-computed MCMC parameter posteriors (found in ad_ode_demo_posterior.pkl
).
 As stated in the Results, the inputs and outputs we selected for the surrogate
 were:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
inputs:
\series default
 age_baseline (sampled uniformly between 60–90 years), binary APOE4_status
 (Bernouli 0.5), and 2-year longitudinal biomarker measurements (amyloid_baseline
, tau_baseline, amyloid_2yr, tau_2yr)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
outputs:
\series default
 2 of the intrinsic ODE kinetic rate parameters (tau_self_dynamic 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{11}$
\end_inset

, amyloid_self_dynamic 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{00}$
\end_inset

, amyloid_drive_tau 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{10}$
\end_inset

), baseline_memory_score, 5-year forecasted memory score (memory_result_5yr),
 and clinical stage scores (clinical_stage_baseline and clinical_stage_5yr).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
SuStaIn-model Surrogate Training
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To train the SuStaIn surrogate model, we constructed a 500-sample dataset
 generated by running pySuStaIn's MCMC classification engine over synthetic
