\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
2.
 The Coupling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We bridged the two models across three core domains using custom mathematical
 transformations as detailed below.
 Note that the metamodeling framework requires to define each coupling as
 having a source with some variables and a target with other variables,
 but the bidirectional property of the probability function and samplings
 makes this a matter of pure notation which helps to distinguish the two
 sides we couple.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
1.
 Subtype-Drive Velocity/Tau Dynamics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Source (SuStaIn): 
\series default
Probabilities of the different subtypes (prob_subtype_0, prob_subtype_1,
 prob_subtype_2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Target (ODE):
\series default
 Tau self dynamic rate 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{11}$
\end_inset

 (tau_self_dynamic)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We defined a velocity_modifier_score transform that generates a modifier
 score 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 using a weighted (
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

) sum of the different subtype probabilities (
\begin_inset Formula $P$
\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S=\sum_{i=0}^{2}\left(P_{i}\times W_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Where we set the weights such that the sum is equal to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S=\left(P_{0}\cdot1.0\right)+\left(P_{1}\cdot(-0.3)\right)+\left(P_{2}\cdot0.5\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice that the weights for subtypes 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{0},W_{2}$
\end_inset

 are positive to indicate their relation to a different higher rate of Tau
 spread, whereas the weight for subtype 1 (the classic lymbic) 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{1}$
\end_inset

 is negative to indicate a relation to a slower rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We wanted the link to have a linear directional potential, such that higher
 probabilities for subtypes 0 and 2 favor higher Tau rates, and a higher
 probability for subtype 1 will favor a lower rate.
 Since the sampling and inference algorithms on the joint distribution use
 log-likelhioods and logarithms of the probabilities, we chose the link
 coupling constraint formula to be
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
log\ p\left(\text{Tau\_Dynamic\_Coupling}|\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right) & =log\ \phi_{1}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{\sigma_{1}}\times\left(\text{tau\_self\_dynamic}\times S\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

 Where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}=2.0$
\end_inset

 is the magnitude modifier - a larger 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1}$
\end_inset

corresponds to a weaker reward/punishment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since the log score is added to the other log probabilities, when comparing
 between two tau_self_dynamic it is clear that a higher rate with a negative
 modifier score 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 punishes the model whereas a positive score S rewards it, and vice versa
 for a slower rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
2.
 Disease Stage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Source (SuStaIn):
\series default
 The calculated Tau expected_stage weighted by the subtype probabilities
 (prob_subtype_0, prob_subtype_1, prob_subtype_2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Target (ODE):
\series default
 The continuous single clinical_stage
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to translate the SuStaIn's Tau expected stage, which is on a discrete
 range of 0-21 to the continous variable in the ODE that tracks the clinical
 stage continuously and is range of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0.0,2.0\right]$
\end_inset

 we had to define a transformation.
 Since the stage is simply a measure of how progressive the Tau spread in
 the patient's brain and not a direct measure of the clinical stage, different
 subtypes can have different clinical stages that are derived from the same
 expected stage.
 So our transformation took the subtype probabilities into consideration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then we linked the clinical stage of the ODE to the transformed Tau expected
 stage using a gaussian connection to reward outputs that sustain the relation
 we defined.
 The transformation and the gaussian link are detailed below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 be the SuStaIn's Tau expected_stage.
 We defined two logistic progression curves targeting a max clinical severity
 of 2.0.
 One for Subtype 1 (limbic) and one for subtypes 0,2 which had more similar
 characteristics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Progression curve for Subtype 1
\series default
: rises faster at lower stages (midpoint anchor 10.0), since this subtype
 is related to a faster clinic decline in earlier Tau stages.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{limbic}\left(x\right)=\frac{2.0}{1+e^{-\alpha\left(x-10.0\right)}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Progression curve for Subtypes 0, 2: 
\series default
rises slower, reaching dementia at higher stages (midpoint anchor 15.0).
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{Atypical}\left(x\right)=\frac{2.0}{1+e^{-\alpha\left(x-15.0\right)}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=0.4$
\end_inset

 is a hyperparameter for the progression rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The final expected clinical stage which is the transformation result is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{Expected Clinical Stage (ECS)}=P\left(S_{1}\right)\cdot C_{limbic}\left(x\right)+\left(P\left(S_{0}\right)+P\left(S_{2}\right)\right)\cdot C_{Atypical}\left(x\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
And thus the final coupling link is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
p\left(\text{clinical\_stage}|\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right) & =\phi_{2}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_{2}^{2}}}exp\left(-\frac{\left(\text{ECS}-\text{expected\_stage}\right)^{2}}{2\sigma_{2}^{2}}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where we set 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{2}=0.2$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once again, since the sampling and inference use logs, we get that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
log\ \phi_{2}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)=-\frac{1}{2\sigma_{2}^{2}}\left(\text{ECS}-\text{expected\_stage}\right)^{2}+Constant
\]

\end_inset

And it is easy to see that the more the two variables are similar, the smaller
 the punishment to the probability assessment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
3.
 Subtype Prior
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Sources (ODE): 
\series default
Disease progression parameters and results (APOE4_status, tau_self_dynamic,
 memory_cognitive_test_result).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Targets (SuStaIn):
\series default
 Subtype probabilities (prob_subtype_0, prob_subtype_1, prob_subtype_2)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we wanted to incorporate the patient's clinical history and charateristics
 as another prior to set the probabilities of the Tau trajectory subtypes.
 Using known empirical associations (carrying APOE4 gene and earlier severe
 memory deficit favors the limbic subtype, rapid decline and favors the
 other subtypes), we calculated scores for each subtype group: 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{limbic}$
\end_inset

 for subtype 1, and 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{Atypical}$
\end_inset

 for subtypes 0,2.
 These scores were then passed through a softmax function to generate a
 probability distribution that acts as a constraint on the SuStaIn mode's
 own subtype predictions - to which we linked using a gaussian link.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, we map the continuous rate of tau spread tau_self_dynamic 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{11}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1)$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V_{norm}=1-e^{-v_{11}}
\]

\end_inset

Next, we clip the memory test score memory_cognitive_test_result 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 to ensure it is within 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,1\right]$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
M_{norm}=clip\left(M,0,1\right)
\]

\end_inset

Then, using also the binary APOE4_status 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, we calculated the scores for limbic and atypical phenotypes.
 Notice that a higher memory score corresponds to a more damaged memory,
 therefore it is added to the limbic score:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
S_{limbic} & =\alpha_{1}A+\alpha_{2}\left(1-V_{norm}\right)+\alpha_{3}M_{norm}\\
S_{Atypical} & =\alpha_{1}\left(1-A\right)+\alpha_{2}\left(V_{norm}\right)+\alpha_{3}\left(1-M_{norm}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset

where we set 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}=\alpha_{2}=\alpha_{3}=1$
\end_inset

 hyperparameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{RawScores}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $RS=\left[S_{Atypical},S_{limbic},S_{Atypical}\right]$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, we set the softmax:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
CoupledP\left(Subtype_{i}\right)=\frac{exp\left(\beta\cdot RS[i]\right)}{\sum_{j=0}^{2}exp\left(\beta\cdot RS[j]\right)}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=1$
\end_inset

 is a temperature hyperparameter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This result is linked to the SuStaIn's original prediction for the probabilities
 using a gaussian just like for the transformed Tau expected stage, so overall
 we get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
p\left(\text{prob\_subtype\_i}|\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right) & =\phi_{3_{i}}\left(\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{ODE},\boldsymbol{\Theta}_{SuStaIn}\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi\sigma_{3}^{2}}}exp\left(-\frac{\left(\text{CoupledP\left(Subtype_{i}\right)}-\text{prob\_subtype\_i}\right)^{2}}{2\sigma_{3}^{2}}\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where we set 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{3}=0.25$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

